.\" Manual page for lsldb(1) — the Sakura Studios LSL debugger front-end.
.\" Copyright (c) 2026 Sakura Studios, IKE. Licensed under the MIT License.
.SH NAME
lsldb \- interactive, gdb-like debugger for LSL scripts running under slemu(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsldb
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-
.RI < slemu " " args ... >
.IR script.lslbc
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsldb
is the Sakura Studios interactive debugger for the Linden Scripting
Language. It is a front-end for
.BR slemu (1):
on startup it spawns a
.B slemu
child with the
.B \-\-debug
flag, attaches to its JSON-line debug protocol over a pair of pipes,
and presents a familiar
.BR gdb (1)-style
read-eval-print loop on the controlling terminal.
.PP
Every argument after the literal
.B \-\-
separator is forwarded verbatim to the
.B slemu
child, so the full suite of
.B slemu
options
.RB ( \-\-config ,
.BR \-\-commands ,
.BR \-\-http\-fixture ,
.BR \-\-volume ,
etc.) is available inside a debug session without any wrapper
syntax.
.PP
.B lsldb
itself is purely a thin REPL plus a source cache: it knows nothing
about LSL semantics, and instead asks
.B slemu
to set breakpoints, step, evaluate variables, list frames and dump
the region snapshot. World events emitted by the simulator are
echoed live between prompts so you can see chat, money and
touch traffic alongside the debugger output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-slemu " PATH"
Use
.I PATH
as the
.B slemu
binary. The default is to search
.IR ./slemu ,
.IR ./sakura-slemu/slemu ,
.IR ../sakura-slemu/slemu ,
.IR ../../sakura-slemu/slemu ,
.IR /usr/local/bin/slemu ,
and finally
.BR slemu
on
.BR $PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-source " PATH"
Register an LSL source file with the source cache so that the
.B list
command and stop-point context display can show source lines around
the current statement. May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
Separator. Every following argument is passed to the
.B slemu
child unchanged, including the final
.I script.lslbc
positional.
.SH "REPL COMMANDS"
The following commands are recognised at the
.B (lsldb)
prompt. Most accept the abbreviation shown in parentheses.
.TP
.BR run " (" r ),  " continue " (" c )
Start or resume execution until the next stop event.
.TP
.BR step " (" s ),  " next " (" n )
Step one source statement.
.TP
.BI break " FILE" : LINE
.TQ
.BI break " LINE"
Set a breakpoint. Without a file, any source file whose name matches
the current script is acceptable.
.TP
.BI delete " N"
Remove breakpoint
.IR N .
.TP
.B info
List active breakpoints and catchpoints.
.TP
.BI catch " KIND"
Stop when the next event of
.I KIND
fires. Recognised kinds include
.BR chat ,
.BR money ,
.BR dialog ,
.BR state_change ,
and
.BR http_out .
.TP
.BI uncatch " N"
Remove catchpoint
.IR N .
.TP
.BR print " (" p ") " \fINAME\fR
Evaluate and print the named variable in the current frame.
.TP
.B locals
List the current frame's locals with types and values.
.TP
.B globals
List the script's globals with types and values.
.TP
.BR backtrace " (" bt )
Show the active call frames.
.TP
.BR list " (" l )
Show source context around the current line.
.TP
.BI source " PATH"
Register an additional source file at run time (equivalent to
.BR \-\-source ).
.TP
.B snapshot
Dump the full region state (avatars, balances, KVP, listens,
inventory) as a JSON object.
.TP
.B events on|off
Toggle the live echo of world events between prompts.
.TP
.BR help " (" h ", " ? )
Show the in-REPL command list.
.TP
.BR quit " (" q )
Detach, terminate the
.B slemu
child, and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The simplest possible interactive session: compile a script with
.BR lslc (1)
and step through it. Everything after
.B \-\-
is forwarded to
.BR slemu (1),
so the script file goes at the end:
.PP
.RS
.B lslc \-c greeter.lsl
.br
.B lsldb \-\- greeter.lslbc
.RE
.PP
Register one or more LSL source files so the
.B list
command and stop-point banner can show source context. Without
.BR \-\-source ,
.B lsldb
still works but only shows file and line numbers:
.PP
.RS
.B lsldb \-\-source greeter.lsl \-\- greeter.lslbc
.RE
.PP
Point at a specific
.B slemu
build (for example, a debug build sitting next to your in-tree
checkout) and pass slemu options through. A multi-avatar debug
session is just a matter of forwarding
.B \-\-config
and
.B \-\-commands
through the separator:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B lsldb \-\-slemu ./build\-debug/slemu \-\-source club_vendor.lsl \e
.B "       \-\- \-\-config worlds/club.cfg \-\-commands scenarios/buy_one.cmd \e"
.B "          club_vendor.lslbc"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Drive
.B lsldb
non-interactively from a shell. Because the REPL reads commands one
per line from standard input, scripted sessions \(em including CI
smoke tests \(em can be written as a shell here-document. The example
below sets a breakpoint, runs to it, prints a variable, and quits:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B printf '%s\en' \e
.B "    'break 4'     \e"
.B "    'continue'    \e"
.B "    'print msg'   \e"
.B "    'quit'        \e"
.B "  | lsldb \-\-source greeter.lsl \-\- greeter.lslbc"
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The debug session completed normally; the user issued
.B quit
or the inferior exited on its own.
.TP
.B 2
Driver error: no script supplied, the
.B slemu
binary could not be found or executed, or the debug pipes could not
be created.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR script .lslbc
SLBC bytecode produced by
.BR "lslc \-c" .
.TP
.I LSL source files
Registered through
.B \-\-source
or the
.B source
REPL command; used purely for displaying source context.
.TP
.B slemu
Spawned as a child process. The binary is located via
.B \-\-slemu
or the search list described above.
.SH BUGS
The
.B \-\-source
matching is intentionally loose: a source file matches a script if
either path is a substring of the other. In monorepos with several
scripts of the same basename you may need to register only the file
you intend to inspect.
.PP
.B lsldb
forwards SIGINT to itself (interrupting the REPL), not to the
.B slemu
child; use the
.B quit
command, not Ctrl-C, to leave a session cleanly.
.PP
